{{also|Klick}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/klɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-klick.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-klick.oga|Audio (US)}}
* {{homophones|en|click|clique}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪk}}

===Etymology 1===
* Most likely a pseudo-condensed pronunciation of {{m|en|kilometer}}, being a suitably short word emphasizing {{m|en|k}}+{{m|en|l}} instead of /keɪ/ alone (the same principle as with military [[spelling alphabet]]s, but also clearly distinguished from {{m|en|kilo}} (/kiːloʊ/) for {{m|en|kilogram}}).
* Possibly onomatopoeic of the sound of a military odometer.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|military}} A [[kilometer]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|first=Robert J.|last=Sawyer|authorlink=Robert J. Sawyer|title=Hominids|text=an asteroid between one and three kilometers wide had slammed into the ground at fifteen '''klicks''' per second.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|usually|in the plural}} [[kilometre|Kilometres]] per [[hour]].

=====Usage notes=====
Though kilometers are not commonly used to measure distance in the USA, {{m|en||klick}} is commonly used by the US & UK military, which use the metric system almost exclusively in order to facilitate communication with allied forces. (In other English-speaking countries, civilians often say "k" or "k.m." instead.)

